C01|N01|6.NS.C.6.c|classification|Coordinate axes, origin, quadrants and scale; no slope needed.|Interpret coordinate signs and axis order.|Which quadrant contains (-3,4)?|Quadrant II.|Swap the coordinates to test axis-order reasoning.
C02|C01|6.NS.C.6.c|ordered_pair|Read and plot points on labeled, scaled coordinate planes.|Represent an ordered pair with the correct horizontal and vertical displacement.|Plot (-2,3) on a coordinate plane.|(-2,3): two units left and three units up.|Change axis scales to distinguish reading marks from reading values.
C03|A01|6.EE.C.9|reasoning|Input and output roles come from the modeling question.|Identify which quantity is treated as the input.|Distance depends on time at a fixed speed. Which quantity is the independent variable?|Time, under the stated model.|Reverse the modeling question to show that the role is contextual.
C04|A09,C03|6.EE.C.9,8.F.A.1|table|Input-output tables with stated domains and units.|Construct values from a rule without confusing inputs and outputs.|For y=2x+1, give y when x=0,1,2.|[1,3,5]|Check one substitution separately from table organization.
C05|C03,C04|8.F.A.1,HSF.IF.A.1|classification|Exactly one output for each input; repeated outputs permitted.|Decide whether a relation is a function and name any offending input.|Is {(1,2),(1,3),(2,3)} a function from its first coordinate?|No; input 1 has outputs 2 and 3.|Contrast repeated inputs with repeated outputs.
C06|C05,A05|HSF.IF.A.1,HSF.IF.A.2|reasoning|Function notation means an output, not multiplication by f.|Interpret function notation in context.|If f maps hours to distance in kilometers, what does f(3)=18 mean?|After 3 hours the modeled distance is 18 kilometers.|Compare f(3) with 3f and with the input 18.
C07|C06,A09|HSF.IF.A.2|scalar|Evaluate a defined function at an allowed input.|Evaluate and interpret an output without solving for the input.|For f(x)=x^2-3x, find f(-2).|10|Contrast evaluation with solving f(x)=-2.
C08|C05,N13|HSF.IF.B.5|structured_response|Domains and ranges from rules, tables, graphs and contexts; restrictions explicit.|State input and output sets for the defined situation.|A trip lasts 3 hours at 5 km/h, with d=5t. Give the time domain and distance range.|{"domain":"[0,3] hours","range":"[0,15] kilometers"}|Compare a continuous time interval with a discrete counting domain.
C09|C02,Q02|HSF.IF.B.4|structured_response|An intercept is a point; a zero is an input with output zero.|Identify zeros and both intercept types.|Find the x- and y-intercepts of y=2x-6.|{"x_intercept":[3,0],"y_intercept":[0,-6]}|Contrast x=0 with y=0 and distinguish a point from its coordinate.
C10|C04,C02,A08|HSF.IF.C.9,8.F.A.2|model|Connect representations of the same relationship without assuming linearity.|Translate a verbal relationship into a rule and a table.|A tank starts with 10 liters and gains 3 liters each minute. Write a rule and the values at 0,1,2 minutes.|V=10+3t; values 10,13,16 liters.|Distinguish initial value from change per interval.
C11|C07,I01|HSF.IF.C.7.b|scalar|Select the correct branch including endpoint inclusion.|Evaluate a piecewise function using its condition.|Let f(x)=x+1 for x<2 and f(x)=2x for x>=2. Find f(2).|4|Probe the boundary value separately from interior values.
C12|C11,C02|HSF.IF.C.7.b|graph_spec|Piecewise linear and step graphs; holes and closed endpoints explicit.|Construct a graph that preserves branch conditions.|Graph f(x)=0 for x<1 and f(x)=2 for x>=1.|Horizontal y=0 left of x=1 with an open point at (1,0); horizontal y=2 for x>=1 with a closed point at (1,2).|Separate branch evaluation from endpoint graphing.
C13|N02,C07,C02|HSF.IF.C.7.b|structured_response|Basic absolute-value graphs and translations; solving absolute-value inequalities belongs to H01.|Interpret absolute value as distance and locate a graph's minimum.|For f(x)=abs(x-2), give the vertex and minimum value.|{"vertex":[2,0],"minimum":0}|Contrast an input shift with an output shift.
C14|C07,N04,N06|HSF.IF.B.6|scalar|Average change over an interval; not instantaneous change or an assumption of linearity.|Compute change in output divided by change in input.|For f(x)=x^2, find the average rate of change from x=1 to x=3.|4|Contrast the quotient of changes with the quotient of endpoint values.
C15|C07,C02|HSF.BF.B.3|ordered_pair|Basic translations, reflection and simple vertical scaling of familiar functions; general family transformations remain H04.|Track the image of a point under a specified function transformation.|The graph of f contains (1,3). Where is the corresponding point on g(x)=f(x-2)+4?|(3,7)|Distinguish an input translation from the sign appearing inside the function.
C16|C07,Q06|HSF.BF.B.4.a|equation|Inverse meaning and nonzero-slope linear inverses; composition and nonlinear restrictions remain H03.|Reverse a simple input-output rule and check the reversal.|Find the inverse of f(x)=3x+2 over the real numbers.|f_inverse(x)=(x-2)/3|Contrast inverse function with reciprocal.
C17|C10|HSF.IF.C.9|structured_response|Compare familiar functions across tables, graphs, rules and descriptions; family-specific prerequisites conditional.|Compare relevant properties rather than matching surface notation.|A table for f has f(0)=3,f(1)=5,f(2)=7 and is known linear. Let g(x)=3x+1. Compare initial values and rates.|{"larger_initial":"f","f_rate":2,"g_rate":3,"larger_rate":"g"}|Separate comparing initial values from comparing rates.
C18|X10,X11,C02,C08|HSF.IF.C.7.b|graph_spec|Basic real square-root and cube-root graphs; distinguish their domains.|Construct a root-function graph and state its real domain.|Graph y=sqrt(x), giving its domain and four exact points.|Domain x>=0; points (0,0),(1,1),(4,2),(9,3); increasing in the first quadrant.|Contrast the square-root domain with the cube-root domain, which allows negative inputs.
C19|C07,E04|HSF.BF.A.1.b|expression|Arithmetic combinations of familiar function rules on their common domain; composition remains H03.|Combine outputs pointwise and retain the common domain.|Let f(x)=x+2 and g(x)=3x. Write h(x)=f(x)+g(x).|4*x+2|Contrast adding outputs at the same input with composing one function into another.
L01|C04,N04|8.F.A.3,HSF.LE.A.1.b|classification|Equal additive output changes over equal input intervals.|Recognize constant rate from a table.|For x=0,1,2,3 the outputs are 5,8,11,14. Is the relationship linear, and what is the rate?|Yes; rate 3 per input unit.|Use unequal input intervals to test whether raw differences are sufficient.
L02|C02,N04,N06|8.EE.B.6,HSF.IF.B.6|scalar|Slope between distinct points; vertical-line case addressed in L03.|Compute slope with the same coordinate subtraction order.|Find the slope through (-1,2) and (3,10).|2|Contrast rise/run with run/rise and inconsistent subtraction order.
L03|L02|8.EE.B.6,HSF.IF.B.6|classification|Positive, negative, zero and undefined slope.|Interpret horizontal and vertical special cases.|What is the slope of a line through (2,-1) and (2,5)?|Undefined; the change in x is zero.|Contrast zero rise with zero run.
L04|C09|HSF.IF.B.4,8.F.B.4|ordered_pair|The y-intercept is the value or point at input zero.|Read a y-intercept and distinguish it from slope.|Give the y-intercept point of y=-3x+7.|(0,7)|Probe the meaning of setting x equal to zero.
L05|L02,L04,C02|HSF.IF.C.7.a|graph_spec|Nonvertical lines in slope-intercept form; scaled axes supported.|Graph a line using the intercept and a rate.|Graph y=-2x+3 with two exact points.|Line through (0,3) and (1,1).|Check slope direction separately from intercept placement.
L06|L01,L02,Q06|HSF.LE.A.2,8.F.B.4|equation|Linear tables with nonzero or zero initial input; do not assume all tables are linear.|Determine rate and initial value from a known linear table.|A linear table contains (1,5),(2,8),(3,11). Write y as a function of x.|y=3*x+2|Contrast the first table output with the value at input zero.
L07|L05,L06|HSF.LE.A.2|equation|Read coordinates from an actual labeled graph; coordinates supplied in the anchor remove image ambiguity.|Write a line rule using graph-derived points.|A line's graph passes through (0,-1) and (2,3). Write its equation.|y=2*x-1|Distinguish a graph-reading error from equation construction.
L08|L02,Q06|HSF.LE.A.2|equation|Two distinct points; vertical cases require x=c rather than y=mx+b.|Write a line equation and verify both points.|Write the equation of the line through (2,1) and (4,5).|y=2*x-3|Use one point to find the intercept after computing slope.
L09|L05,Q13|HSA.CED.A.4,HSF.IF.C.7.a|equation|Slope-intercept, point-slope and standard forms represent the same line.|Rewrite a linear equation without changing its solution pairs.|Rewrite 2x+3y=12 in slope-intercept form.|y=(-2/3)*x+4|Check division of every term when isolating y.
L10|L05,N14|8.F.B.4,HSF.LE.A.1.b|reasoning|Proportional relationships have zero initial value and constant ratio.|Distinguish a fixed fee from a proportional rate.|Is C=5+2n proportional to n? Explain.|No; the fixed term 5 prevents a constant C/n ratio and the graph does not pass through the origin.|Contrast constant difference with constant ratio.
L11|L03,C02|HSF.IF.C.7.a,HSA.REI.D.10|structured_response|Horizontal y=c and vertical x=c; only the former is a function of x.|Identify equations of horizontal and vertical lines.|Name the vertical line through (4,-2) and the horizontal line through it.|{"vertical":"x=4","horizontal":"y=-2"}|Contrast the coordinate that stays fixed on each line.
L12|L05,L13|HSF.IF.C.9|model|Compare linear models across representations; contextual units required.|Interpret an intersection as a comparison threshold.|Two plans cost A=10+2n and B=4+3n. When do they have equal cost?|n=6; both cost 22.|Compare equality at the threshold with which plan is cheaper on each side.
L13|L05,A10|HSF.LE.B.5,HSS.ID.C.7|reasoning|Interpret slope and intercept with compatible units and realistic domains.|Connect coefficients to quantities in a model.|For C=12+4h, explain 12 and 4.|12 dollars is the fixed charge; 4 dollars per hour is the hourly rate.|Ask for units to distinguish a rate from a total.
L14|L01,L05|8.F.A.3,HSF.LE.A.1|reasoning|Linearity requires constant change per equal input interval, not visual roughness.|Test a proposed linear relationship.|For x=0,1,2, outputs are 1,2,5. Could these points lie on one line?|No; the successive output changes are 1 and 3 for equal input changes.|Distinguish nonlinearity from an irregularly spaced input table.
S01|Q02,C02|8.EE.C.8.a,HSA.REI.C.6|classification|A simultaneous solution satisfies every equation.|Test an ordered pair in a system.|Does (2,3) solve x+y=5 and 2x-y=1?|Yes; both equations are true.|Compare satisfying one equation with satisfying the whole system.
S02|S01,L05|HSA.REI.C.6|ordered_pair|Graphical intersections of two lines; exact versus approximate coordinates distinguished.|Identify and verify the intersection.|Find the intersection of y=x+1 and y=-x+5.|(2,3)|Separate graph-reading precision from algebraic verification.
S03|S01,Q09|HSA.REI.C.6|ordered_pair|Substitution in two-variable linear systems; replace the entire expression.|Solve through substitution and verify both equations.|Solve y=2x+1 and x+y=10.|(3,7)|Probe grouping when the substituted expression has several terms.
S04|S01,Q09|HSA.REI.C.5,HSA.REI.C.6|ordered_pair|Elimination as addition of equivalent equations; entire equations combined.|Solve and justify the elimination operation.|Solve x+y=7 and x-y=1.|(4,3)|Contrast adding equations with adding only selected terms.
S05|S04,E07|HSA.REI.C.5,HSA.REI.C.6|ordered_pair|Scaling every term before elimination.|Choose and apply useful equation multipliers.|Solve 2x+3y=13 and 3x-2y=0.|(2,3)|Check scaled constants as well as coefficients.
S06|S01,Q12|HSA.REI.C.6|classification|Unique intersection, parallel distinct lines and coincident lines.|Interpret a contradictory or redundant system.|Classify 2x+y=4 and 4x+2y=9.|No solution; the second left side is twice the first but the constants conflict.|Contrast equal slopes with identical equations.
S07|S01,A10,M02|HSA.CED.A.3,HSA.REI.C.6|model|Contextual systems, defined variables and feasible interpretations.|Build a system and interpret the result in context.|Ten tickets cost 68 dollars. Adult tickets cost 8 dollars and child tickets 5 dollars. Find the counts.|a+c=10 and 8a+5c=68; a=6 adults and c=4 children.|Separate model construction from the chosen solving method.
S08|I09,S01|HSA.CED.A.3,HSA.REI.D.12|graph_spec|Intersection of half-planes with all boundary rules preserved.|Identify a complete feasible region.|Describe the region satisfying x>=0, y>=0 and x+y<=6.|Closed triangle including its boundary with vertices (0,0),(6,0),(0,6).|Test a point against every constraint rather than one inequality.
S09|S01|HSA.REI.C.7,HSA.REI.D.11|point_set|One line and one quadratic; graph or symbolic solution route; no general nonlinear-system claim.|Find every real intersection and verify both relations.|Solve y=x+2 and y=x^2.|[[-1,1],[2,4]]|Contrast solving the substituted quadratic with checking the resulting y-values.
X01|N18|6.EE.A.1,8.EE.A.1|scalar|Whole-number exponents as repeated multiplication; not a product of base and exponent.|Interpret and evaluate a power.|Evaluate 2^5.|32|Contrast exponent notation with multiplying 2 by 5.
X02|X01,N05|8.EE.A.1,HSA.SSE.A.1.a|structured_response|Placement of a negative sign and parentheses; reciprocal calculation belongs to X08.|Distinguish the base from a leading negative sign.|Evaluate -3^2 and (-3)^2.|{"negative_of_square":-9,"square_of_negative":9}|Change parentheses only to isolate the scope of the exponent.
X03|X01|8.EE.A.1|expression|Products with a common nonzero base where required by later variants.|Use repeated multiplication to justify addition of exponents.|Simplify x^3*x^4.|x^7|Contrast multiplying like bases with multiplying unlike bases.
X04|X03,N06|8.EE.A.1|expression|Common-base quotients with nonzero base; positive exponent difference initially.|Subtract exponents and retain domain restrictions.|Simplify x^7/x^3 and state the original restriction.|x^4, with x!=0.|Contrast a simplified formula with its original domain.
X05|X03|8.EE.A.1|expression|Power of a power; integer exponents.|Multiply exponents rather than add them.|Simplify (x^3)^2.|x^6|Compare a product of powers with a power of a power.
X06|X03,N10|8.EE.A.1|expression|Powers applied to every factor or quotient component.|Distribute a power over a product correctly.|Expand (2x)^3.|8*x^3|Distinguish cubing the coefficient from multiplying it by the exponent.
X07|X04|8.EE.A.1|reasoning|a^0=1 for a nonzero; 0^0 is outside the item family.|Explain the zero-exponent rule using a quotient.|Why is 5^0=1?|5^3/5^3 equals 1 and also equals 5^(3-3).|Check the nonzero-base condition before extending the rule.
X08|X07,N10|8.EE.A.1|fraction|Negative integer exponents indicate reciprocals; zero base excluded.|Rewrite a negative exponent as a reciprocal.|Evaluate 2^(-3).|1/8|Contrast a negative exponent with a negative value.
X09|X08,N20|8.EE.A.3,8.EE.A.4|expression|Scientific notation uses 1<=abs(a)<10 and an integer exponent.|Normalize a decimal in scientific notation.|Write 0.00072 in scientific notation.|7.2*10^(-4)|Separate moving the decimal from choosing the exponent sign.
X10|N15,N13|8.EE.A.2,HSN.RN.B.3|interval|Principal square roots; exact and approximate values distinguished.|Bound a nonperfect square root without calling it a two-valued function.|Between which consecutive integers is sqrt(50)?|(7,8)|Contrast the principal square root with solving x^2=50.
X11|X01,N05,N13|8.EE.A.2|scalar|Real cube roots of positive and negative values; exact perfect cubes and estimates.|Use the inverse of cubing with the correct sign.|Evaluate cbrt(-125).|-5|Contrast an odd root of a negative number with an even root.
P01|A02,X01|HSA.APR.A.1|classification|Polynomial expressions in one variable with nonnegative integer exponents.|Distinguish a polynomial from a variable-denominator expression.|Is 3x^2-2x+1 a polynomial? Is 1/x a polynomial in x?|The first is a polynomial; 1/x is not.|Contrast numerical coefficients with a variable in a denominator.
P02|P01|HSA.APR.A.1,HSA.SSE.A.1.a|structured_response|Degree after simplification; leading coefficient includes its sign.|Identify degree and leading coefficient.|Give the degree and leading coefficient of -4x^3+2x-7.|{"degree":3,"leading_coefficient":-4}|Present terms out of order to test reliance on visual position.
P03|P01,E03|HSA.APR.A.1|expression|Add polynomials by matching exponents; core tasks through quadratic degree.|Add polynomial expressions without combining unlike powers.|Simplify (2x^2+3x-1)+(x^2-5x+4).|3*x^2-2*x+3|Isolate matching powers from signed coefficient addition.
P04|P03,E08|HSA.APR.A.1|expression|Subtract an entire polynomial, including every sign.|Subtract polynomials correctly.|Simplify (3x^2+x-2)-(x^2-4x+5).|2*x^2+5*x-7|Compare correct collection after the subtraction group is removed.
P05|E07,X03|HSA.APR.A.1|expression|A monomial times each polynomial term.|Combine distribution with exponent rules.|Expand -2x(3x^2-x+4).|-6*x^3+2*x^2-8*x|Separate exponent combination from sign multiplication.
P06|E06,P05|HSA.APR.A.1|expression|Binomial products including all cross terms.|Expand a product using a justified area or distributive model.|Expand (x+3)(x-2).|x^2+x-6|Check the middle term independently of the first and last terms.
P07|P06|HSA.APR.A.1|expression|Optional extension to longer products and degree above two.|Apply distribution beyond a memorized binomial routine.|Expand (x+1)(x^2-x+1).|x^3+1|Test whether the method generalizes when there are more than four partial products.
P08|P06,X01|HSA.APR.A.1,HSA.SSE.A.2|expression|Square-of-sum, square-of-difference and difference-of-squares identities.|Expand a special product and justify its middle term.|Expand (x-4)^2.|x^2-8*x+16|Contrast squaring a binomial with squaring its terms separately.
P09|P03,P04|HSA.APR.A.1|expression|Simplification preserves distinct powers; identity restrictions unchanged.|Collect polynomial terms without merging different exponents.|Simplify 2x^2+3x-x^2+4x.|x^2+7*x|Contrast like powers with visually similar variable terms.
F01|E10,P01|HSA.SSE.A.2|expression|Greatest common numerical and variable factors; complete extraction required.|Identify and factor the greatest common monomial.|Factor 12x^3+18x^2 completely using a common factor.|6*x^2*(2*x+3)|Separate coefficient GCF from the minimum shared exponent.
F02|F01,N02|HSA.SSE.A.2|expression|Negative common factors with equivalent remaining signs.|Choose a negative common factor and preserve the product.|Factor -3x^2+6x with a negative greatest common monomial.|-3*x*(x-2)|Re-expand to distinguish a sign convention from an incorrect identity.
F03|N18,N05|HSA.SSE.B.3.a|structured_response|Signed integer factor pairs; product and sum both matter.|Find factors satisfying two constraints.|Find two integers whose product is -12 and whose sum is 1.|4 and -3.|Check the product and sum separately before accepting a pair.
F04|F03,P06|HSA.SSE.B.3.a|expression|Monic integer trinomials that factor over the integers.|Factor a monic trinomial and verify by expansion.|Factor x^2+x-12.|(x+4)*(x-3)|Use the signed factor-pair probe F03 to distinguish factoring structure from integer facts.
F05|F04,P05|HSA.SSE.B.3.a|expression|Nonmonic quadratics with an integer factorization; irreducible cases acknowledged.|Factor and check both middle cross terms.|Factor 6x^2+11x+3.|(3*x+1)*(2*x+3)|Check the combined cross-term coefficient rather than only the first and last products.
F06|P08,N15|HSA.SSE.A.2,HSA.SSE.B.3.a|expression|A difference of two squares; sums of squares do not use the same real identity.|Recognize and factor a difference of squares.|Factor 9x^2-16.|(3*x-4)*(3*x+4)|Contrast 9x^2-16 with 9x^2+16.
F07|P08,F04|HSA.SSE.A.2,HSA.SSE.B.3.a|expression|Perfect-square trinomials require the matching middle term.|Recognize and factor a square trinomial.|Factor x^2-10x+25.|(x-5)^2|Vary the middle coefficient while holding the two square terms fixed.
F08|P06|HSA.SSE.A.2|reasoning|Verification must recover every term of the original expression.|Reject or confirm a factorization by expansion.|Is x^2+5x+6=(x+1)(x+6)? Justify.|No; the proposed product expands to x^2+7x+6.|Distinguish matching constant terms from full equivalence.
F09|Q05,Q02,A04|HSA.REI.B.4.b|solution_set|Zero-product property applies only when the product equals zero.|Set each factor equal to zero and report all solutions.|Solve (x-3)(x+2)=0.|[-2,3]|Contrast a zero product with a product equal to a nonzero number.
